# Symmetry Decor — Mobile Performance Optimization Plan

After a thorough review of the entire game source code, I've identified **7 performance bottlenecks** that would significantly impact mobile devices. They are ordered by estimated impact (highest first).

---

## 🔴 Critical Issues

### 1. Unnecessary Arcade Physics on Every Entity

**File:** [entity.js](file:///c:/work/mci-cognitive-games/src/game/symmetry-decor/entity/entity.js#L3-L8)

**Problem:** Every `Entity` extends `Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite` and calls `scene.physics.add.existing(this)` in its constructor. This means **every single grid cell** (up to 36 on a 6x6 grid) gets a full Arcade Physics body — velocity, acceleration, collision checks, etc. — even though the game is a drag-and-drop puzzle with **zero physics interactions**.

On a 6x6 grid, you create:
- 36 cell entities (each with a physics body)
- Up to 18 draggable entities (each with a physics body)  
- Up to 18 blocker entities (each with a physics body)

That's **~72 active physics bodies** being stepped every frame for no reason.

**Impact:** The Arcade Physics engine runs `step()` on all bodies every frame. On mobile GPUs/CPUs, this is one of the heaviest per-frame costs.

**Fix:** Switch `Entity` base class from `Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite` to `Phaser.GameObjects.Sprite`. Remove `scene.physics.add.existing(this)`. The `SpriteRenderer.syncPhysicsBody()` calls can be safely removed since physics bodies are not needed.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This is the single biggest performance win. It removes ~72 unnecessary physics body updates per frame.

